Specifically, the libc interposition machinery in
Sources/MallocInterposerC/src/interposer-darwin.c and
Sources/MallocInterposerC/src/interposer-unix.c (and the corresponding
declarations in Sources/MallocInterposerC/include/interposer.h) is derived
from SwiftNIO's allocation-counter test framework
(IntegrationTests/allocation-counter-tests-framework):

  * On Darwin: the DYLD_INTERPOSE macro and the replacement_* / DYLD_INTERPOSE
    wiring.
  * On Linux: the dlsym(RTLD_NEXT, ...) symbol resolution cached in atomic
    globals, the recursive-malloc-during-dlsym BSS bump allocator, and the
    JUMP_INTO_LIBC_FUN macro.

This code has been substantially modified by Ordo One AB — most notably a
per-thread TLS counting model in place of SwiftNIO's global atomic counters,
header-prefix per-allocation size tracking with pointer classification, a
small/large size-class split, runtime enable/disable/reset gating, and
alignment-honouring aligned-allocation paths. SwiftNIO's socket and
file-descriptor-tracking hooks were removed. See the per-file headers for a
summary of the changes.
